|> Exercise
    handle=_what_seq_sin_Bx_plus_C_

    What sequence of transformations of the plane
    maps the graph of

    $$
    x \ra \sin(x)
    $$

    onto the graph of

    $$
    x \ra \sin(Bx + C)
    $$

    ...?

    |> Solution
        We give a “physics-based answer”.
       
        As discussed in Exercise >>_four_particles_, the function

        $$
        t \ra \cos(Bt + C)
        $$

        describes, among others, the $x$-coordinate of a
        particle going clockwise around the unit circle
        at $B$ radians per unit time,
        starting at $C$ radians clockwise from $(1, 0)$
        at time $t = 0$.
       
        By the same token,
        $$
        t \ra \cos(t)
        $$
        describes (among others) the $x$-coordinate of a
        particle going clockwise around the unit circle 
        at ONE radian per unit time, starting at ZERO radians
        clockwise from $(1, 0)$ at time $t = 0$.
       
        To get from the $1$-$0$-graph to the $B$-$C$-graph
        we need to do first things first, and start
        by translating the graph $y = \cos(t)$ until the
        point $(C, \cos(C))$ is at the origin, 
        i.e., the
        first thing is to translate the graph left by $C$
        units, so that the value $\cos(C)$ is at $t = 0$:

        |> Image
            src=images/svg_ch5_ex_cos_Bx_plus_C_step_1.svg
        
        Now that we have the value we want at $t = 0$,
        the next step is to “accelerate” the graph by a
        factor $B$—i.e., the second step is to
        _horizontally compress by a factor $B$_, to
        reflect a particle going $B$ radians per second,
        not $1$ radian per second—here illustrated with
        $B = 5.5$:
        
        |> Image
            src=images/svg_ch5_ex_cos_Bx_plus_C_step_2.svg

        And what goes for $\cos$ goes for $\sin$ (or 
        indeed for any function $f$, even though the
        physical interpretation is no longer at hand);
        but to recapitulate, the answer is the
        following sequence of two steps:
        
        |> BoxedText
            class=px-0
            style=padding-top:1.5rem;padding-bottom:1.5rem;
            __1. horizontally translate the graph
            |> br 
            by $-C$__

            __2. horizontally compress the resulting graph
            by a factor $B$__

        |> TildeDivider
            style=margin-top:-1em
        
        |> SolutionNote

            The first step corresponds to
            
            __phase adjustment__
            
            in the language of physicists (i.e., when
            a sinusoid is horizontally translated such that
            it crosses the $y$ axis at a desired
            location of its wave), while the second step
            can be described as
            
            __angular frequency adjustment__
            
            or just
            
            __frequency adjustment__
            
            if we imagine playing with the dial of a
            synthetizer and watching some blue wave modulate.
            (Recall that “angular frequency” is a synonym
            of “angular velocity”, meaning
            _radians per unit time_.)

            Note that if you adjust the angular frequency
            _before_ adjusting the phase, the phase
            will need to be adjusted by a different amount,
            in order to account for the already-changed
            angular frequency; in that case, the sequence
            of transformations becomes:
            
            |> BoxedText
                class=px-0
                style=padding-top:1.5rem;padding-bottom:1.5rem;

                __1. horizontally compress the resulting graph
                by a factor $B$__

                __2. horizontally translate the graph by $-C/B$__

            ...where the translation is now $-C/B$ instead
            of $-C$ to account for the fact that the graph
            has already been compressed when the translation
            is effected. (In fact, this alternate sequence of
            transformations and the $C/B$ term are directly 
            related to the fact that

            $$
            Bx + C
            $$

            can be written

            $$
            B(x + C/B)
            $$

            believe it or not.)
